    $tc_master$## I. The Problem of the Twentieth Century

Every century is a fiction of the calendar, but the twentieth resists even that convenience. Historians cannot agree on when it happened. Eric Hobsbawm, in the most influential single volume on the period, argued for a "short twentieth century" running from 1914 to 1991 — from the guns of August to the lowering of the Soviet flag — a coherent epoch defined by the rise and fall of the October Revolution and the world wars that framed it. Others counter with a "long century": Giovanni Arrighi traced an American cycle of capitalist hegemony reaching back into the 1870s; historians of empire and globalization see the years 1870–1914 as the true opening act, the first age of globalized capital whose collapse the century then spent decades repairing. Chinese historiography periodizes around 1911, 1949, and 1978; for much of Africa and Asia the pivotal dates are 1947, 1960, or the fall of apartheid in 1994, and the "short century" can look suspiciously like a European civil war given planetary billing. The dispute is not pedantry. To choose a periodization is to choose a protagonist — capitalism, ideology, empire, or technology — and this article will keep more than one in view.

What no periodization can dissolve is the century's central paradox. By any measure of organized violence it was the worst in human history: the two world wars, the Holocaust and the century's other genocides, the Stalinist and Maoist famines and terrors, and the wars of decolonization together killed something on the order of 150–200 million people — the margin of error alone exceeds the population of most countries. Yet by any measure of human welfare it was the best: global life expectancy roughly doubled, from around 32 years in 1900 to about 66 by 2000; child mortality collapsed; smallpox, which killed perhaps 300 million people in the century's first eight decades, was eradicated in 1980 — the only human disease ever deliberately extinguished; literacy became a majority condition of humankind for the first time; and world population, defying every Malthusian forecast, nearly quadrupled from 1.6 to 6.1 billion *while* average real incomes multiplied several-fold. The century of Auschwitz and the Gulag was also the century of penicillin, the Green Revolution, and the franchise extended — in law if not always in fact — to women and to the colonized. A serious history of the twentieth century must hold both ledgers open at once and ask how they are related; the uncomfortable answer, pursued throughout what follows, is that the same instruments — the powerful bureaucratic state, industrial technology, mass mobilization, and the sciences — wrote both.

## II. The World of 1900: A First Globalization and Its Illusions

The century opened on a world more economically integrated, in some respects, than it would be again until the 1980s. Capital, goods, and above all people moved with astonishing freedom: some 55–60 million Europeans emigrated overseas between 1850 and 1914; the gold standard linked currencies; a Briton, as Keynes famously recalled, could order the products of the whole earth to his doorstep and considered this state of affairs "normal, certain, and permanent." It was also a world owned by empires. In 1900 roughly a fifth of the earth's land surface answered to the British crown — a quarter by the empire's 1920s peak; the recent partition of Africa (formalized at Berlin, 1884–85) had left only Ethiopia and Liberia nominally independent; the Qing empire, humiliated serially since the Opium Wars and about to suppress the Boxer uprising with eight foreign armies on its soil, survived on sufferance. European hegemony rested on an industrial and military gap — the Maxim gun, the steamship, quinine — and on an intellectual superstructure of racial hierarchy and civilizational mission that most educated Europeans regarded as science.

Yet the belle époque's confidence was already being falsified at its edges. Japan's defeat of Russia in 1905 — the first modern victory of an Asian over a European power — electrified anticolonial intellectuals from Cairo to Hanoi and helped detonate revolution in Russia itself; revolutions in Persia (1906), the Ottoman Empire (1908), Mexico (1910), and China (1911) announced that the periphery would not wait for permission. Within Europe, the very forces that seemed to guarantee progress were dissolving the old order's foundations: mass literacy and mass suffrage created mass politics that aristocratic diplomacy could no longer insulate; socialist parties became the largest in Germany; the new mass press discovered that jingoism sold. Norman Angell argued in 1910 that economic interdependence had made great-power war irrational, and he was right; the century would demonstrate, repeatedly, that irrationality was no barrier.

## III. 1914–1918: The Seminal Catastrophe

The American diplomat George Kennan called the First World War "the great seminal catastrophe of this century," and the description has become standard because the causal chains from Sarajevo run everywhere: to Lenin's sealed train, to the Versailles order and its resentments, to the militarization of the Middle East's map, to fascism, and — through the second war the first made possible — to the nuclear age. How the catastrophe began remains the discipline's longest-running argument. The Versailles settlement asserted German war guilt; interwar revisionists spread the blame; Fritz Fischer's *Griff nach der Weltmacht* (1961) scandalized West Germany by documenting Berlin's expansionist war aims and willingness to risk continental war, relocating responsibility firmly to Germany's leadership; and the centenary's most influential book, Christopher Clark's *The Sleepwalkers* (2012), redistributed agency again among a cast of risk-taking statesmen in Vienna, Belgrade, St. Petersburg, and Paris who walked open-eyed into disaster without willing it. The debate matters beyond attribution: it is a proxy for the deepest question in historical explanation — structure versus contingency. Were alliance systems, mobilization timetables, and imperial rivalries a machine that some crisis was bound to trip, or did particular men make particular, avoidable choices in July 1914? The best current scholarship declines the dichotomy: structures loaded the weapon; choices fired it.

What the war *was* proved more transformative than how it began. It inaugurated **total war** — the mobilization of entire economies, populations, and imaginations. States that had taken perhaps 5–10 percent of national income now commandeered half of it; they rationed food, conscripted labor, directed industry, propagandized systematically, and policed dissent — building, in four years, the administrative capacities that every subsequent project of the century, welfare state and police state alike, would inherit. Women entered munitions factories and, in Britain, Germany, the United States and elsewhere, won the vote in the war's immediate wake — less a reward than an acknowledgment that legitimacy now required the mobilized. The fighting itself — Verdun and the Somme in 1916 consumed roughly two million casualties between them for gains measured in kilometers — destroyed the nineteenth century's equation of war with glory and progress with Europe. Some ten million soldiers died; the influenza pandemic of 1918–19 then killed more people worldwide than the war itself, on most estimates. And the war was global from its first weeks: fought in Africa, the Middle East, and the Pacific; fed by perhaps four million colonial soldiers and laborers from India, Indochina, Africa, and China whose service sharpened every question about the empires they served.

{{image: Battle of the Somme | The Western Front, 1916: industrial war measured its gains in yards and its cost in hundreds of thousands, and buried the nineteenth century's equation of war with glory.}}

The settlement institutionalized the instability. Four empires — Romanov, Habsburg, Hohenzollern, Ottoman — dissolved; Wilson's principle of national self-determination, applied in Europe and pointedly denied elsewhere (the Egyptian, Indian, Korean, and Chinese delegations and petitioners of 1919 went home empty-handed, a formative disillusionment Erez Manela has called "the Wilsonian moment"), produced a belt of quarrelsome successor states each with its own aggrieved minorities; the Anglo-French mandate carve-up of the Ottoman Arab lands drew borders still bleeding a century later; and the reparations regime imposed on a Germany left intact but humiliated furnished the ideal grievance. Keynes predicted the consequences in 1919. He was accurate on the economics; nobody's imagination reached the politics.

## IV. The Interwar Crisis: Three Futures Contend

The Russian Revolution of October 1917 split the century. Lenin's seizure of power converted a doctrine into a state, and thereafter world politics was a three-cornered contest — liberal capitalism, communism, fascism — each claiming to own the future, each defining itself against the others. The Bolshevik state pioneered the century's most consequential political technology: the single mobilizing party fused with the state, commanding economy, media, and terror. Stalin's "revolution from above" after 1928 — forced collectivization and breakneck industrialization — built the industrial base that would absorb Hitler's invasion, at the cost of a famine (most murderously in Ukraine, the Holodomor of 1932–33, killing on the order of four million) and a Terror whose archives, opened after 1991, ended the older Western debate about scale: roughly 700,000 executions in 1937–38 alone, and a Gulag through which some 18 million passed. The historiographical war over whether Stalinism fulfilled or betrayed Leninism — continuity versus rupture — remains unresolved because it is also a war over whether the communist project was corrupted or was corrupting.

Fascism was the second novelty: a revolutionary politics of the right — nationalist, anti-liberal, anti-Marxist, aestheticizing violence and mass mobilization — first in Mussolini's Italy (1922), then in a family of imitators, and in its most radical form in Germany. The scholarly consensus, articulated by Robert Paxton and others, treats fascism not as a coherent doctrine but as a *process* feeding on liberalism's failures; and the failure that fed it best was economic. The Depression that began in 1929 was the deepest in industrial history — US output fell by roughly a quarter, German unemployment reached a third of the workforce — and it discredited economic orthodoxy (the gold standard, balanced budgets, laissez-faire) with a thoroughness no argument could have achieved.

{{image: Great Depression | The Great Depression: the deepest slump in industrial history discredited economic orthodoxy and, where democracy was young and the last war's losers many, proved lethal to democracy itself.}}

The democracies that survived did so by reinventing themselves: Roosevelt's New Deal and Sweden's social-democratic compromise pioneered the managed, welfare-cushioned capitalism that would define the postwar West, and Keynes's *General Theory* (1936) gave the reinvention its theory. Where liberal institutions were young and losers of the last war numerous, the Depression was lethal to democracy itself: by 1939, most of the democracies created in 1919 were gone. Hitler's appointment as chancellor in January 1933 — the result not of electoral majority but of conservative elites' fatal confidence that they could use him — remains the century's most consequential single miscalculation.

## V. 1937–1945: The Second War and the Rupture of Civilization

The Second World War is best dated not from the German invasion of Poland in September 1939 but from Japan's full-scale invasion of China in July 1937 — a reminder that the Eurocentric frame truncates even the century's central event. It was the largest single occurrence in human history by almost any metric: some 70–85 million dead, a majority of them civilians — a proportion inverted from 1914–18 and itself a definition of what "total war" had come to mean. The Soviet Union lost on the order of 27 million people and, with China, absorbed the demographic weight of the war; roughly four of every five German soldiers killed died on the Eastern Front, a fact postwar Anglo-American memory long obscured. The war fused every technology and administrative capacity the century had yet developed — radar, operational research, code-breaking (and with it the birth of the computer), strategic bombing that erased Hamburg, Dresden, and sixty Japanese cities, and finally the Manhattan Project, industrial physics harnessed to produce the weapons used on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945. Whether those bombings ended the war or the Soviet declaration of war did, and whether their primary audience was Tokyo or Moscow, remains a live historiographical dispute (Hasegawa's *Racing the Enemy* is the standard revisionist statement); what is not disputed is that humanity exited the war owning the means of its own extinction.

{{image: Atomic bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki | August 1945: with the atomic bomb, humanity exited the war owning the means of its own extinction.}}

At the war's center stands the Holocaust: the systematic murder of some six million European Jews, alongside Roma, the disabled, Soviet prisoners, and others, in a program that moved from persecution to mobile mass shooting on Soviet territory in 1941 to the industrialized killing centers of occupied Poland. The event has become the century's moral reference point, and its explanation the discipline's gravest responsibility. The older "intentionalist" reading — a straight line from Hitler's ideology to Auschwitz — has been complicated by "functionalist" scholarship tracing how policy radicalized cumulatively through bureaucratic competition and local initiative within an ideological framework Hitler set; Christopher Browning's *Ordinary Men* (1992) demonstrated how conformity, deference, and brutalization turned middle-aged reserve policemen into shooters, an unsettling finding precisely because it declined to make the perpetrators monsters. Zygmunt Bauman pressed the largest claim: that the Holocaust was not a relapse into barbarism but a possibility *of* modernity itself — bureaucracy, classification, distance, and technique put in the service of a biological utopia. One need not accept the thesis whole to accept its discipline: the century's worst crime was committed by one of its most educated societies, and no theory of progress survives that fact unamended.

The war's end reorganized the planet. Europe, self-ruined, forfeited world primacy to the two continental powers its wars had summoned — the United States, holding perhaps half of world industrial production in 1945, and the Soviet Union, holding Eastern Europe. The United Nations, the Bretton Woods institutions, the Nuremberg and Tokyo tribunals, the Genocide Convention (1948) and Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) constituted an attempt to institutionalize the lessons; the expulsion of some 12 million Germans from Eastern Europe, the Partition of India (1947, perhaps a million dead and 15 million displaced), and the Palestinian Nakba (1948) demonstrated in real time that the age of unmixing peoples by force was not over but cresting.

## VI. The Cold War: An Armed Peace and Its Historians

The Soviet-American confrontation that congealed between 1945 and 1948 was a new kind of conflict: global in scope, ideological in essence, waged by every means short of direct great-power war — because the thermonuclear revolution (H-bombs by 1952–55, intercontinental missiles by decade's end) had made such war self-abolishing. "Mutual assured destruction" was less a strategy than a condition, and the Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 — thirteen days during which, we now know, the decision loop ran through a Soviet submarine officer named Vasili Arkhipov — is the closest documented approach to the precipice.

{{image: Cuban Missile Crisis | October 1962: for thirteen days the Cuban Missile Crisis brought the thermonuclear powers to the closest documented approach to the precipice.}}

Its historiography has moved in generations: an "orthodox" school blaming Soviet expansionism; a Vietnam-era "revisionism" (William Appleman Williams and heirs) blaming American economic empire; and a "post-revisionist" synthesis, rearmed after 1991 by Soviet archives, in which John Lewis Gaddis and others found ideology mattering more, and Stalin worse, than the revisionists allowed — while the newest wave (Odd Arne Westad's *The Global Cold War*) relocated the story altogether, from Berlin to the Third World, where the "cold" war killed millions: Korea (c. 3 million), Vietnam and Indochina (c. 2–3 million), Afghanistan, Angola, Central America, and the anti-communist massacres in Indonesia in 1965–66 (at least 500,000 dead) that barely trouble Western memory.

Within the rival blocs, the postwar generation built the century's most successful social orders — and its most oppressive. The capitalist West's *trente glorieuses* (roughly 1945–1973) delivered the fastest broad-based growth in economic history: Marshall aid, Bretton Woods stability, cheap oil, catch-up technology, and an explicit class compromise — strong unions, high marginal taxes, and welfare states from the NHS to Scandinavian social democracy — produced full employment and, uniquely in the century, *falling* inequality. Japan's and West Germany's "miracles" and later the East Asian tigers extended the model. The Soviet bloc industrialized and educated at speed and put the first satellite (1957) and first human (1961) into orbit, but suppressed Hungary in 1956 and Czechoslovakia in 1968, walled Berlin in 1961, and settled after Stalin into the stagnation that Vaclav Havel would anatomize as living within the lie. Meanwhile Mao's China ran the century's most lethal peacetime experiment: the Great Leap Forward (1958–62) produced a famine now credibly estimated at 30–45 million deaths — the largest in recorded history — followed by the Cultural Revolution's decade of organized chaos.

## VII. The End of Empire: The Century's Largest Transformation

Seen from most of humanity, the century's principal event was neither world war nor Cold War but the liquidation of European empire. In 1945 nearly a third of the world's population lived under colonial rule; by 1980, formal empire was essentially finished — the most rapid geopolitical transformation in history, tripling the roster of sovereign states. Its motors were several and compounding: the world wars' destruction of imperial prestige and finance (Japan's 1941–42 conquests had shattered the myth of white invincibility in Asia); the mobilization of colonial soldiers and workers who returned with claims; mass nationalist movements led by a remarkable generation — Gandhi's satyagraha in India (independence 1947), Nkrumah in Ghana (1957, the door-opener for the seventeen African independences of 1960 alone), Ho Chi Minh, Nasser, Kenyatta; and the new superpowers' shared, if instrumentalized, anti-colonialism. Decolonization was rarely tidy and often ferocious: the French wars in Indochina and Algeria (1954–62, with torture systematized and perhaps hundreds of thousands to over a million dead), the British suppression of Mau Mau in Kenya with its camps, the Portuguese wars into 1974–75, and southern Africa's long endgame — Zimbabwe 1980, Namibia 1990, and the fall of apartheid with Mandela's election in 1994, the century's most redemptive single scene.

{{image: Nelson Mandela | Nelson Mandela, whose 1994 election closed the century's long story of empire and racial rule on its most redemptive note.}}

The successor states inherited borders drawn elsewhere, export economies built for the metropole, and expectations no treasury could meet. The Bandung conference (1955) and the Non-Aligned Movement asserted a "Third World" project of solidarity and development; its economic arm demanded a New International Economic Order (1974) and largely did not get it, as the debt crisis of the 1980s delivered many new states into the structural-adjustment custody of the IMF instead — "neocolonialism," in Nkrumah's coinage, or the discipline of markets, in the lenders'. Historiographically, decolonization has moved from the margins to the center: postcolonial scholarship (Said's *Orientalism*, 1978; the Subaltern Studies school) rewrote the questions, and the empirically stunning finding of recent decades — from the Belgian Congo's rubber terror to Britain's destroyed Kenya files — is how much imperial violence was archived out of the record. The "global turn" in the discipline is, in large part, the twentieth century's own decolonization arriving in the seminar room.

## VIII. The Other Revolutions: Society, Science, and the Self

Beneath the political narrative ran transformations that touched more lives than any treaty. **Demography and medicine**: antibiotics (penicillin mass-produced from 1943), vaccines, oral rehydration, and DDT-era vector control collapsed death rates decades before birth rates followed, producing the population explosion, its urbanization (a world 13% urban in 1900 approached half by 2000), and then the demographic transition's second act — the Pill (1960) and fertility declines that remade family life. **Women**: over the century, women moved — unevenly, incompletely — from legal minors to voters (New Zealand first in 1893, Switzerland not until 1971, and onward), to workers in majority, to controllers of their own fertility; the two waves of feminism, suffragist and post-1963 liberationist, rank among the century's most successful social movements, and the transformation of gender roles may be, at the scale of daily life, its deepest single change. **Rights revolutions**: the American civil rights movement (Brown 1954, Montgomery 1955, the Acts of 1964–65) dismantled legal apartheid in the world's leading democracy and exported a grammar of nonviolent protest that anti-apartheid, democracy, and dignity movements adapted worldwide. **The sciences** rewrote the human position: relativity and quantum mechanics in the century's first quarter, the DNA double helix (1953) and the code's near-complete reading by 2000, plate tectonics, the Big Bang confirmed (1965), and a computing lineage from Turing's 1936 paper through ENIAC to the microprocessor (1971), the personal computer, and the web (1989–91) — the infrastructure of the *next* century built, almost inadvertently, by this one. And in **culture**, the century invented mass mediated experience itself — cinema, radio, recorded music, television — instruments equally of Hollywood, the BBC, and Goebbels; no previous century could propagandize, or entertain, at the speed of light.

## IX. The Hinge of the 1970s and the Neoliberal Turn

The postwar golden age ended in the early 1970s with a compound fracture: Nixon closed the gold window (1971), ending Bretton Woods; the OPEC embargo (1973) quadrupled oil prices; and "stagflation" — inflation with stagnation, which reigning Keynesianism said could not happen — did to the postwar consensus what 1929 had done to its predecessor. The beneficiaries were the waiting counter-doctrines of Hayek and Friedman: the elections of Thatcher (1979) and Reagan (1980), the Volcker interest-rate shock, privatization, deregulation, weakened unions, and liberated capital flows inaugurated the market-first order later named neoliberalism — restoring price stability and dynamism at the cost of the inequality curve turning back upward, where it has trended since. Simultaneously and more consequentially still, Deng Xiaoping's reforms from 1978 began moving the largest nation on earth from Maoist autarky into the world market, initiating the greatest mass exit from poverty in history and the geoeconomic story of the twenty-first century. The same hinge decade produced the human-rights breakthrough (Helsinki Accords 1975, Amnesty's Nobel 1977 — Samuel Moyn has argued this, not 1948, is when human rights became the "last utopia"), the environmental awakening (Silent Spring 1962 maturing into Earth Day 1970 and the first climate warnings to governments by decade's end), and the Iranian Revolution (1979), which announced that secular modernization was not history's only direction.

Then, with a speed that embarrassed every forecaster, the Cold War ended. Gorbachev's attempt to reform Soviet socialism (glasnost, perestroika) instead unraveled it; the captive nations took the opening — Poland's Solidarity elections, the Berlin Wall breached on November 9, 1989, the velvet and not-so-velvet revolutions — and in December 1991 the Soviet Union dissolved itself, an empire ending without a great-power war, something essentially without precedent.

{{image: Fall of the Berlin Wall | November 1989: the Berlin Wall is breached, and within two years an empire ends without a great-power war — something essentially without precedent.}}

Whether the causes were Reagan's pressure, oil prices, imperial overstretch, Gorbachev's choices, or the moral secession of civil society is the last great Cold War debate; the honest answer is again conjunctural. The 1990s coda mixed triumphalism (Fukuyama's "end of history," the globalization boom, the web) with prompt refutation: genocide in Rwanda (1994, some 800,000 dead in a hundred days, with the institutional lessons of 1948 standing idle) and in Bosnia (Srebrenica, 1995) closed the century as it had run, with the gap between norms proclaimed and protection delivered.

## X. The Ledger and the Lesson

How, then, to hold the century's two ledgers together? The tempting narratives fail symmetrically. A story of progress cannot metabolize Auschwitz, Kolyma, and Hiroshima as incidents; a story of catastrophe cannot explain doubled lifespans, vanquished smallpox, and the peaceful end of 1989–91. The defensible synthesis is instrumental: the twentieth century massively amplified human capability — through the bureaucratic state, industrial technique, mass communication, and organized science — and amplified capability is morally symmetrical. The same state capacity ran the ration book and the deportation train; the same chemistry fixed nitrogen for bread (Haber, 1909) and for explosives and gas (Haber again); the same physics lit cities and leveled them. What differed, decisively, were institutions and ideas: where power was accountable, plural, and constrained, capability compounded into welfare; where a single party, leader, or doctrine captured the amplified state, the century's worst pages were written. That is not a triumphalist conclusion — the constrained-power societies built empires, dropped the atomic bombs, and outsourced ample violence — but it is the pattern the evidence supports, and it is the twentieth century's principal bequest of political knowledge, purchased at the stated price. The century also left its successor two unsigned invoices: nuclear arsenals that made extinction a standing policy option, and a carbon-driven transformation of the atmosphere, measured continuously since 1958 and understood by governments from the 1980s, whose bill arrives now.

---

## Further Reading

- **Eric Hobsbawm, *The Age of Extremes* (1994).** The indispensable single volume on the "short twentieth century" — best read against its critics.
- **Tony Judt, *Postwar* (2005).** The standard history of Europe after 1945.
- **Odd Arne Westad, *The Global Cold War* (2005).** Relocates the Cold War from Berlin to the Third World, where it killed millions.
- **Christopher Clark, *The Sleepwalkers* (2012).** The most influential recent account of how Europe walked into war in 1914.
- **Ian Kershaw, *To Hell and Back* (2015).** A lucid narrative history of Europe from 1914 to 1949.
- **Timothy Snyder, *Bloodlands* (2010).** On the Hitler–Stalin killing zone between Berlin and Moscow.
- **Christopher Browning, *Ordinary Men* (1992).** How conformity and brutalization turned middle-aged policemen into killers.
- **Erez Manela, *The Wilsonian Moment* (2007).** The colonial world's disillusionment with self-determination after 1919.
- **Barry Eichengreen, *Globalizing Capital* (1996).** The gold standard, Bretton Woods, and the international monetary system.
- **Thomas Piketty, *Capital in the Twenty-First Century* (2013).** For the century's inequality U-curve.
- **Zygmunt Bauman, *Modernity and the Holocaust* (1989).** The provocation that the Holocaust was a possibility of modernity itself.
- **Samuel Moyn, *The Last Utopia* (2010).** Argues human rights became the reigning moral language only in the 1970s.
- **Vaclav Smil, *Creating the Twentieth Century* (2005).** The technical foundations everything else stood on.$tc_master$,
